1
00:00:01,121 --> 00:00:01,581
Hello.

2
00:00:01,761 --> 00:00:06,284
My project asks how MP3 and Opus compression
change speech

3
00:00:06,484 --> 00:00:10,236
and the internal representations of Wav2Vec2,

4
00:00:10,286 --> 00:00:14,549
and which changes are associated with
recognition errors.

5
00:00:14,989 --> 00:00:19,322
The motivation is simple: a signal can be
distorted

6
00:00:19,372 --> 00:00:23,474
without necessarily causing the recogniser to
make more mistakes.

7
00:00:25,695 --> 00:00:27,977
The experiment starts with LibriSpeech speech.

8
00:00:28,257 --> 00:00:33,000
Each utterance is encoded with MP3 or Opus at
different bitrates,

9
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:34,241
then decoded.

10
00:00:34,501 --> 00:00:39,855
The same pretrained Wav2Vec2 Base recogniser
processes every condition

11
00:00:39,905 --> 00:00:42,506
using greedy CTC decoding.

12
00:00:42,947 --> 00:00:45,448
I compare the original and compressed signals

13
00:00:45,608 --> 00:00:50,191
using spectral measurements, hidden-state
drift and word error rate.

14
00:00:50,892 --> 00:00:56,616
The full study uses the same 500 selected
utterances per subset

15
00:00:56,676 --> 00:00:58,096
across all conditions.

16
00:01:00,938 --> 00:01:03,220
Now I will show the signal-processing code.

17
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:06,773
This short demonstration uses the two
utterances selected

18
00:01:06,823 --> 00:01:10,666
for the report’s Opus 8 kbps case study.

19
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:14,710
The script loads the fixed Wav2Vec2 model,

20
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:20,254
then calls FFmpeg to encode and decode the
speech.

21
00:01:20,635 --> 00:01:24,498
It runs the recogniser on the original and
compressed signals

22
00:01:24,578 --> 00:01:27,020
and calculates the word error rates.

23
00:01:29,795 --> 00:01:32,125
This terminal recording shows an actual run

24
00:01:32,175 --> 00:01:34,255
of the two-utterance demonstration.

25
00:01:34,635 --> 00:01:36,676
It displays the reference transcript,

26
00:01:36,776 --> 00:01:39,916
the recogniser’s output for the original WAV

27
00:01:40,117 --> 00:01:42,737
and its output after Opus compression.

28
00:01:43,297 --> 00:01:46,118
The word error rates are computed in that run.

29
00:01:46,398 --> 00:01:51,349
The spectral distance and layer-12 drift shown
as saved values

30
00:01:51,399 --> 00:01:52,959
come from the final report,

31
00:01:53,179 --> 00:01:56,720
rather than being recalculated by this short
demonstration.

32
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,080
First, listen to the robust case:

33
00:02:00,461 --> 00:02:05,161
the original reference followed by its Opus
reconstruction.

34
00:02:05,482 --> 00:02:08,162
Then I will play the failure case in the same
order.

35
00:02:10,163 --> 00:02:12,204
[Robust - Original WAV] Their assumed character
changed

36
00:02:12,265 --> 00:02:15,207
[Robust - Original WAV] with their changing
opportunities or necessities.

37
00:02:16,148 --> 00:02:18,169
[Robust - Opus 8 kbps] Their assumed character
changed

38
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:21,192
[Robust - Opus 8 kbps] with their changing
opportunities or necessities.

39
00:02:22,373 --> 00:02:24,624
[Failure - Original WAV] The wood flamed up
splendidly

40
00:02:24,674 --> 00:02:29,757
[Failure - Original WAV] under the large brewing
copper, and it sighed so deeply.

41
00:02:31,078 --> 00:02:33,309
[Failure - Opus 8 kbps] The wood flamed up
splendidly

42
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:38,482
[Failure - Opus 8 kbps] under the large brewing
copper, and it sighed so deeply.

43
00:02:39,963 --> 00:02:44,545
In the robust case, the recogniser gets both
versions correct.

44
00:02:44,746 --> 00:02:47,497
In the failure case, the original is correct,

45
00:02:47,547 --> 00:02:50,869
but the Opus transcript changes “flamed up” to
“flame ot”.

46
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:55,832
The spectrogram shows that much of the
high-frequency content is lost.

47
00:02:56,113 --> 00:03:00,075
These selected cases illustrate individual
outcomes.

48
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:05,771
The full experiment shows why signal and
recognition measures

49
00:03:05,871 --> 00:03:07,672
need to be considered together.

50
00:03:08,132 --> 00:03:12,544
At MP3 24 kbps, the average log-spectral
distance

51
00:03:12,594 --> 00:03:14,974
is about 9.2 dB,

52
00:03:15,535 --> 00:03:19,416
and the retained bandwidth is about 5.8 kHz.

53
00:03:19,756 --> 00:03:23,748
Yet the test-clean word error rate changes

54
00:03:23,798 --> 00:03:27,379
by only 0.02 percentage points,

55
00:03:27,759 --> 00:03:31,780
and the speaker-level confidence interval
includes zero.

56
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:38,403
At Opus 8 and 6 kbps, the encoder switches to
narrowband,

57
00:03:38,703 --> 00:03:42,724
and the recognition loss is larger, especially
on test-other.

58
00:03:44,537 --> 00:03:47,058
I also compare the model’s hidden states.

59
00:03:47,278 --> 00:03:49,258
Before calculating the cosine drift,

60
00:03:49,518 --> 00:03:53,369
each hidden-state dimension is standardised

61
00:03:53,419 --> 00:03:55,399
using a fixed calibration set.

62
00:03:55,779 --> 00:03:58,579
This reduces the influence of anisotropy.

63
00:03:59,019 --> 00:04:03,510
Across conditions per subset, layer-12 drift
tracks

64
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:08,001
the change in word error rate more linearly
than spectral distance.

65
00:04:08,421 --> 00:04:13,201
Pearson correlations are about 0.96 to 0.97,

66
00:04:13,481 --> 00:04:17,602
compared with 0.75 to 0.79.

67
00:04:18,022 --> 00:04:21,643
However, the Spearman rank correlations are
similar.

68
00:04:23,730 --> 00:04:26,861
A different question is which utterances
acquire errors

69
00:04:26,911 --> 00:04:29,112
within a fixed codec condition.

70
00:04:29,592 --> 00:04:33,993
The mean within-condition Spearman association
for layer 12

71
00:04:34,154 --> 00:04:40,676
is only about 0.09 on test-clean and 0.23 on
test-other.

72
00:04:41,076 --> 00:04:43,277
So the association is modest,

73
00:04:43,517 --> 00:04:47,508
and it should not be presented as a reliable
prediction

74
00:04:47,558 --> 00:04:49,519
for each individual utterance.

75
00:04:51,913 --> 00:04:54,554
These are the two examples from the final
report.

76
00:04:54,834 --> 00:04:57,255
Their global spectral distances are similar,

77
00:04:57,496 --> 00:05:00,197
around 10.8 and 10.5 dB,

78
00:05:00,557 --> 00:05:05,680
while their final-layer drifts are 0.042 and
0.129.

79
00:05:06,401 --> 00:05:10,503
The examples were deliberately selected from
different drift ranges

80
00:05:10,643 --> 00:05:12,804
and matched for their signal distortion.

81
00:05:13,164 --> 00:05:15,325
They illustrate the difference,

82
00:05:15,466 --> 00:05:19,167
but do not prove that the pattern holds for
every utterance.

83
00:05:21,555 --> 00:05:24,787
To conclude, compression can alter the speech
signal

84
00:05:24,837 --> 00:05:27,578
before it produces a measurable recognition
loss.

85
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:33,760
My contribution is to link signal distortion,
representation changes

86
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:37,361
and recognition performance using the same
utterances.

87
00:05:38,102 --> 00:05:42,223
EnCodec provides supporting evidence from
another codec family,

88
00:05:42,383 --> 00:05:43,804
but not a causal test.

89
00:05:44,464 --> 00:05:47,745
The study uses one recogniser and read English
speech,

90
00:05:48,065 --> 00:05:51,267
and the final layer is directly connected to
the output.

91
00:05:51,627 --> 00:05:54,718
Further interventions and additional models

92
00:05:54,768 --> 00:05:58,309
will be needed to test the mechanism. Thank
you.
